WEBVTT

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.360
think this Devon landscape is the most beautiful place on earth and to me this

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.390
is a very special farm because it's where I grew up

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:36.040
And it's the only place I've ever really called home.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:45.410
My name is Rebecca Hosking, and I'm from a long line of farmers.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.050
But it was the wildlife here, more than the farming,

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.450
that really fascinated me as a child.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:58.520
And this led me into a career as a wildlife filmmaker.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.000
But now I'm back here to be

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.040
farmer and in very interesting times. An approaching energy crisis will likely force a revolution

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:24.690
in farming and change the British countryside forever. It will affect what we eat, where

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:30.000
it comes from and even the alarming question of whether there will be enough food to be

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:45.030
food to keep us fed. If our farm is to survive, it will have to change. In this film, I'm going to

00:01:45.030 --> 00:02:00.000
find out how to make my family farm in Devon a farm that's fit for the future. People sort of

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:07.470
find out i was brought up on a small south devon farm they always think it i must have had the most

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:15.800
amazing childhood ever when i think back to when i was brought up here i just think of a load of

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:22.340
bloody hard work really we were just small time farmers and with that is involved not much money

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:27.990
and a lot of hard work to the point that it's almost drudgery

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.640
Dad often describes farmers as glorified lavatory attendants.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:42.780
My family, like many farming families up and down the country, wanted something better

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:49.330
for their children, and I was actively encouraged to get out of farming, go and find a job,

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.090
go and make a decent living.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:59.600
So that's what I did, and while I was away pursuing my career,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.070
Dad and my uncle Phil carried on as ever, farming in a pretty traditional way, but now

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.530
it's time for me to come back.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:18.870
The thing is, both Phil and I now, I was going to say we're several years beyond retiring

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.270
age and should have retired, and most farmers have done that.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:30.000
But we've kept the farm going and kept it going as long as we can, trying to keep it

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.150
as we found it, as we sort of inherited it. I'm delighted to think somebody will take

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:43.170
it on now and keep it going, hopefully. But it's not going to be easy because of pressures

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:49.430
of all sorts of things. Food shortages, oil prices going up. It's not going to be easy

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:54.150
at all.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:04:00.000
Many would say, just sell it. That would make more money in a heartbeat than a lifetime of

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.520
the land. But how can I turn my back on somewhere so beautiful and a place that made me who

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:14.290
I am? However, making a living while continuing to preserve all the wildlife on the farm as

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:25.870
dad has done is going to be a major challenge. The inconvenient truth is that this farm despite

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:30.000
being a haven for wildlife is no more sustainable than any

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.870
the other. All the farms I know, including organic ones, are utterly dependent on fossil

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:44.030
fuel, particularly oil. This dependence is dangerous for two reasons. Climate change

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:50.170
we all know about, but there is also growing evidence that the oil we need may soon be

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:59.160
in short supply. Last year's fuel prices hit us badly, and for me it was a bit of a wake-up

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:06.370
I recently learned that those crippling fuel prices may be just a tiny taster of what's

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:12.020
to come as world oil production begins to decline.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:16.800
If there's any truth to this matter, then this will be my biggest challenge in keeping

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.460
our farm going into the near future.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:27.860
So I decided to track down one of the world's most respected authorities on the subject.

